title: When watch or ignore changes
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: reference
summary: Changing mf_watch or mf_ignore re-applies the watcher's coverage at once but never rewrites or deletes a metarecord; what is new needs a reconcile, what left stays frozen.

Changing `mf_watch` or `mf_ignore` does ''not'' modify any existing metarecord. It
changes what happens next:

* the watcher re-applies its coverage ''at once'' — re-placing its per-directory watches in the budget regime, or simply filtering differently in the coverage regime — so events in a newly eligible subtree are captured from then on;
* ''`mf_watch` set to `true`'' on a directory: the files already there have no metarecord yet; a reconcile creates them ([[Running a reconcile]]);
* ''`mf_watch` set to `false`'', or a new pattern now matching tracked files: their metarecords stay exactly as they are, `mfr_path` included, and are no longer updated by the watcher or reconcile ([[Freezing metadata by not watching]]).

A rollback or a redo that brings `mf_watch`/`mf_ignore` rows back or takes them away
re-applies the coverage the same way, once HEAD has landed.

Nothing is lost implicitly. To clean up after a scope change, delete explicitly:

|!Goal |!Query to delete |
|everything under a directory |`mfr_path ->* "/dir"` |
|metarecords whose file was deleted |`mfr_path IS ABSENT` |
|what left the watch scope |a query over the untracked subtree |

(`mf metarecord -q '…' delete`, [[Editing a set of metarecords]].)
